{"task_id": "Mbpp/622", "solution": "def get_median(a, b, n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the median of two sorted lists of the same size using a recursive approach.\n    Assumes lists `a` and `b` are sorted and have length `n >= 1`.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # Helper function to calculate the median of a single sorted array.\n    def _median_single(arr, size):\n        if size % 2 == 1:\n            return float(arr[size // 2])\n        else:\n            return float((arr[size // 2 - 1] + arr[size // 2]) / 2.0)\n\n    # Base cases for recursion\n    if n == 1:\n        return float((a[0] + b[0]) / 2.0)\n    if n == 2:\n        return float((max(a[0], b[0]) + min(a[1], b[1])) / 2.0)\n\n    # Calculate medians of the individual arrays\n    m1 = _median_single(a, n)\n    m2 = _median_single(b, n)\n\n    # If medians are equal, this is the overall median\n    if m1 == m2:\n        return float(m1)\n\n    # Recursive step: reduce the search space by half\n    if m1 < m2:\n        # Median must lie in the upper half of 'a' or the lower half of 'b'.\n        # Discard the first n//2 elements of 'a' and last n//2 elements of 'b'.\n        if n % 2 == 0:\n            # For even n, new subarrays are a[n/2 - 1:] and b[:n/2 + 1]\n            return get_median(a[n // 2 - 1:], b[:n // 2 + 1], n - n // 2 + 1)\n        else:\n            # For odd n, new subarrays are a[n/2:] and b[:n/2 + 1]\n            return get_median(a[n // 2:], b[:n // 2 + 1], n - n // 2)\n    else:  # m1 > m2\n        # Median must lie in the upper half of 'b' or the lower half of 'a'.\n        # Discard the first n//2 elements of 'b' and last n//2 elements of 'a'.\n        if n % 2 == 0:\n            # For even n, new subarrays are a[:n/2 + 1] and b[n/2 - 1:]\n            return get_median(a[:n // 2 + 1], b[n // 2 - 1:], n - n // 2 + 1)\n        else:\n            # For odd n, new subarrays are a[:n/2 + 1] and b[n/2:]\n            return get_median(a[:n // 2 + 1], b[n // 2:], n - n // 2)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/620", "solution": "def largest_subset(nums: list) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the size of the largest subset of numbers where every pair is divisible.\n    Uses dynamic programming (O(n^2) time). Handles zeros so that:\n      - 0 with 0 is treated as divisible,\n      - non-zero is not considered divisible by 0,\n      - non-zero may be divisible by non-zero via modulo.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(nums)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    nums_sorted = sorted(nums)\n    dp = [1] * n  # dp[i] = length of largest divisible subset ending at i\n    max_len = 1\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            is_divisible = False\n            if nums_sorted[j] == 0:\n                # Only allow 0 with 0\n                if nums_sorted[i] == 0:\n                    is_divisible = True\n            else:\n                if nums_sorted[i] % nums_sorted[j] == 0:\n                    is_divisible = True\n\n            if is_divisible and dp[j] + 1 > dp[i]:\n                dp[i] = dp[j] + 1\n\n        if dp[i] > max_len:\n            max_len = dp[i]\n\n    return max_len"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/626", "solution": "def triangle_area(radius):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the area of the largest triangle that can be inscribed in a semicircle.\n    Returns None for invalid inputs like negative or non-numeric radius.\n    \"\"\"\n    try:\n        # Attempt to convert the radius to a float\n        r = float(radius)\n    except (TypeError, ValueError):\n        # If conversion fails (e.g., non-numeric string), return None\n        return None\n\n    # If the radius is negative, return None as per requirements\n    if r < 0:\n        return None\n\n    # For a semicircle with radius R, the largest inscribed triangle has an area of R^2\n    return r * r"}
